      "body": "<p>On September 15, 1830, the Liverpool and Manchester Railway opened ceremonially in England, linking two industrial cities with a scheduled railway worked entirely by steam locomotives. George Stephenson served as chief engineer, while Robert Stephenson and Joseph Locke contributed to the railway's engineering development.</p><p>The opening procession used several locomotive-hauled trains and included prominent political and engineering figures, among them Arthur Wellesley, Duke of Wellington. The railway incorporated major civil-engineering works, including bridges, viaducts, tunnels, and the crossing of Chat Moss.</p><p>The ceremony was overshadowed by the fatal injury of William Huskisson at Parkside, after he entered the path of the approaching Rocket. The accident made the limits of contemporary railway safety unmistakable.</p><p>The opening provided public, operational evidence that steam locomotives could haul passenger trains between Liverpool and Manchester on a purpose-built railway. It helped establish railways as a major form of transport and influenced the spread of railway engineering, but it did not demonstrate that railway travel was universally safe, that Rocket was the final or best locomotive design, or that every railway could be built under identical conditions.</p>",
